WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.907 --> 00:00:08.774
Hi. I'm Sue Biggins,

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:11.444
and it's a privilege to talk to Lee Hartwell today

00:00:11.445 --> 00:00:16.249
about his pioneering work in understanding the regulation of the cell cycle.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:21.220
This of course led to the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2001.

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.556
Just for background, I want to remind everyone

00:00:23.557 --> 00:00:26.192
that the cell cycle is an ordered process

00:00:26.193 --> 00:00:30.897
where the genome and organelles are duplicated and accurately partitioned into daughter cells.

00:00:30.898 --> 00:00:34.467
And it's really one of the most fundamental things for all organisms.

00:00:34.468 --> 00:00:38.337
By studying the morphology of temperature-sensitive mutants in yeast,

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:42.074
Lee Hartwell identified many of the key regulators of the cell cycle.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:46.779
His seminal discoveries included identifying the cell division cycle, or CDC, genes

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.082
that are now known to be universal regulators of the cell cycle

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.719
in organisms from yeast to frogs to humans.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.123
Perhaps the best known of these genes is cdc28,

00:00:57.124 --> 00:00:59.525
which is the master regulatory kinase

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:03.129
that controls the progression of cells through the start of the cell cycle.

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:06.065
Hartwell is also credited for discovering cell cycle checkpoints,

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:08.301
such as the DNA damage checkpoint.

00:01:08.302 --> 00:01:12.505
Checkpoints are signals that let the cell know if a step in the cell cycle is not completed,

00:01:12.506 --> 00:01:15.174
and then generate a cell cycle arrest.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.276
And Lee, it's really fun to be talking here in Seattle,

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:21.047
where you did your groundbreaking work that led to the Nobel Prize.

00:01:21.048 --> 00:01:22.782
Thanks for doing this.

00:01:22.783 --> 00:01:24.283
HARTWELL: My pleasure.

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:28.888
BIGGINS: So, to get started, Lee, can you tell us what got you started or interested in science?

00:01:28.889 --> 00:01:32.658
HARTWELL: Oh, going back a long ways?

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:38.097
I think the turning point for me was in high school.

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:42.068
I hadn't been a very serious student,

00:01:42.069 --> 00:01:47.707
but I had a great physics teacher,

00:01:47.708 --> 00:01:52.512
who challenged me with hard problems to take home and work on.

00:01:52.513 --> 00:01:55.448
And I just look back on that as a turning point

00:01:55.449 --> 00:01:58.251
where I got interested in science.

00:01:58.252 --> 00:02:02.188
BIGGINS: And what about biology? When did you get interested in biology?

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:05.391
HARTWELL: Not until I was in Caltech.

00:02:05.392 --> 00:02:09.595
As a sophomore, I took a... I was... I was planning to be a physics major,

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:14.300
and I took a course from James Bonner.

00:02:14.301 --> 00:02:21.741
And it was supposed to be sort of a biology... general biology course.

00:02:21.742 --> 00:02:23.976
But as I remember it,

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:29.515
he spent the entire semester ranting and raving about how great DNA was.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:31.784
So, this was about 1957 or so.

00:02:31.785 --> 00:02:35.521
So, it was only a few years after the structure of DNA had been discovered.

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:39.058
And he was so exciting,

00:02:39.059 --> 00:02:43.062
I just changed my major on the spot, and became a biologist.

00:02:43.063 --> 00:02:46.365
BIGGINS: Great. What inspired you to work on the cell cycle?

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.769
HARTWELL: Well, I... looking back,

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:55.641
I think that somewhere I gained the perspective that

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:58.144
scientists ask big questions.

00:02:58.145 --> 00:03:02.014
I suppose that I got that idea at Caltech.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:06.219
But after I'd gotten my graduate PhD,

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:12.458
and was deciding where to go for a postdoc,

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:14.927
and thinking about what I wanted to work on,

00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:17.063
I chose cell division.

00:03:17.064 --> 00:03:20.132
Because, as you say, it's very fundamental to biology.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:23.769
And it seemed like almost nothing was known about it.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:28.708
So, that seemed like an interesting direction to go in.

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:33.779
BIGGINS: And do you want to tell us a little bit about the CDC screen?

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.251
HARTWELL: Well, let me first just mention how we got into yeast.

00:03:38.252 --> 00:03:40.319
BIGGINS: Yeah, I was gonna ask that question.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.687
Everyone asks that question.

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.422
Why yeast?

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:48.794
HARTWELL: Yeah. I wrote a grant to do cell division studies in mammalian cells,

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:50.563
as I had been doing.

00:03:50.564 --> 00:03:53.032
But I knew I didn't wanna do that.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:57.236
And I was really frustrated about what to do.

00:03:57.237 --> 00:04:00.940
And I had about... a period of about three months

00:04:00.941 --> 00:04:04.577
where the equipment I'd ordered was on...

00:04:04.578 --> 00:04:09.682
coming but not arrived yet, when I just had time to think.

00:04:09.683 --> 00:04:14.587
And I remember talking to one of my colleagues about this, Dan Wolf.

00:04:14.588 --> 00:04:18.391
He was from Delbrück's group at Caltech.

00:04:18.392 --> 00:04:20.927
I had worked in Delbrück's group as an undergraduate.

00:04:20.928 --> 00:04:23.229
And he said, well,

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.932
why don't you take... look for a simpler organism?

00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:29.735
And that was the key for me.

00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:32.371
And I immediately went to the library

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:36.576
and started scouring for eukaryotic cells

00:04:36.577 --> 00:04:40.713
where you could do genetics and cell physiology.

00:04:40.714 --> 00:04:43.516
And so that's how I came to yeast.

00:04:43.517 --> 00:04:49.655
And it was really because of a frustration with what I couldn't do with animal cells.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:53.860
BIGGINS: And so that leads me to wonder about looking for temperature-sensitive mutants.

00:04:53.861 --> 00:04:56.162
Why did you make that decision?

00:04:56.163 --> 00:04:59.131
HARTWELL: Well, I wanted the essential genes.

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:03.369
And I had, as an undergraduate, as I said,

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:06.072
worked in Delbrück's group with Bob Edgar.

00:05:06.073 --> 00:05:13.346
And Bob Edgar did a beautiful piece of work on how

00:05:13.347 --> 00:05:19.285
a T4 virus is put together -- 150 proteins or so --

00:05:19.286 --> 00:05:22.955
getting mutations in genes

00:05:22.956 --> 00:05:26.526
that were part of the structure of the virus.

00:05:26.527 --> 00:05:31.297
Now, the problem is a mutant

00:05:31.298 --> 00:05:36.602
that's defective in an essential function for the organism is dead.

00:05:36.603 --> 00:05:39.038
And so you have to make them conditional,

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:42.341
so they'll grow under one condition but not under another.

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:45.545
And Bob had used temperature-sensitive mutants.

00:05:45.546 --> 00:05:51.217
And Horowitz, at Caltech,

00:05:51.218 --> 00:05:53.286
very early in genetics,

00:05:53.287 --> 00:05:55.821
had gotten temperature-sensitive mutants in Neurospora.

00:05:55.822 --> 00:05:58.958
So, I knew about that conditional phenomenon,

00:05:58.959 --> 00:06:01.627
and so that was the obvious way to go.

00:06:01.628 --> 00:06:04.363
BIGGINS: Now, why don't you tell us the story

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:06.933
about the morphologies and how you...

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:10.102
what you were looking for, and how you went about that?

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:13.472
HARTWELL: When I moved to the University of Washington,

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:16.175
the genetics department,

00:06:16.176 --> 00:06:19.412
there was an undergraduate in the laboratory there,

00:06:19.413 --> 00:06:22.915
Brian Reid, who is now at the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Center

00:06:22.916 --> 00:06:25.251
studying esophageal cancer.

00:06:25.252 --> 00:06:30.122
And I suggested to Brian that

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:35.428
he look at some mutants that formed odd morphologies.

00:06:35.429 --> 00:06:40.633
And the question was, once you...

00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:43.569
there was some really interesting work in Paramecium

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:47.306
that showed that if you surgically changed the surface of the cell

00:06:47.307 --> 00:06:51.978
that was inherited.

00:06:51.979 --> 00:06:54.547
And so that was very interesting.

00:06:54.548 --> 00:06:58.150
Some kind of structural inheritance on the surface.

00:06:58.151 --> 00:07:01.354
So, we had mutants that at a high temperature

00:07:01.355 --> 00:07:03.723
became morphologically abnormal.

00:07:03.724 --> 00:07:06.292
And I suggested to Brian that he

00:07:06.293 --> 00:07:09.829
look to see if that was inherited.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.064
So, he would shift them to the high temperature,

00:07:12.065 --> 00:07:13.699
and then shift them back down to the low temperature.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:15.868
And it turned out it wasn't inherited.

00:07:15.869 --> 00:07:18.437
But then we...

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:20.239
he got interested in the question, well,

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:23.009
how do they go from abnormal to normal?

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.812
And so he started taking pictures of them in the microscope.

00:07:26.813 --> 00:07:29.282
And as I remember it,

00:07:29.283 --> 00:07:33.219
immediately we realized how much information there was

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.189
in these photographs about the cell cycle.

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:39.692
And started, then, screening all of our mutants

00:07:39.693 --> 00:07:43.329
for their morphological changes

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.665
when we shifted the temperature.

00:07:45.666 --> 00:07:47.967
BIGGINS: So, another question I had is,

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:51.904
how did you order the cell cycle using your mutants?

00:07:51.905 --> 00:07:55.708
HARTWELL: Well,

00:07:55.709 --> 00:07:58.077
the mutants provided some order

00:07:58.078 --> 00:08:02.148
because, for example, there were mutants that didn't bud;

00:08:02.149 --> 00:08:05.651
there were mutants that didn't make DNA,

00:08:05.652 --> 00:08:07.386
and then everything else stopped;

00:08:07.387 --> 00:08:10.189
there were mutants that didn't divide the nu...

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:12.725
that made DNA, but didn't divide the nucleus;

00:08:12.726 --> 00:08:15.194
and different steps around the cell cycle.

00:08:15.195 --> 00:08:19.999
So, in principle, what the mutants told us

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.668
was that there's basically two cycles.

00:08:22.669 --> 00:08:28.508
When the cell passes the commitment to division

00:08:28.509 --> 00:08:30.643
that we called Start,

00:08:30.644 --> 00:08:34.814
the nuclear cycle is one series of events --

00:08:34.815 --> 00:08:37.617
the DNA synthesis and the division of the nucleus.

00:08:37.618 --> 00:08:40.319
And another series of events has to do with the cell surface,

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.889
which is the formation of the bud,

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:49.529
and then eventually the division of the cell wall at division time.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:52.164
And then these two things, these two pathways,

00:08:52.165 --> 00:08:54.267
have to hook up at the end.

00:08:54.268 --> 00:08:57.737
So, the the pathways and events...

00:08:57.738 --> 00:08:59.906
order of events...

00:08:59.907 --> 00:09:04.110
was largely determined by the phenotypes of the mutants.

00:09:04.111 --> 00:09:07.613
BIGGINS: Can you explain sort of the two big models

00:09:07.614 --> 00:09:10.783
you were trying to distinguish between in terms of the regulation of the cell cycle,

00:09:10.784 --> 00:09:14.253
i.e. the substrate model versus a timing model?

00:09:14.254 --> 00:09:19.192
HARTWELL: So, when the cells stop in the cell cycle

00:09:19.193 --> 00:09:24.430
because something hasn't been completed,

00:09:24.431 --> 00:09:26.599
the question becomes why they stop.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:33.372
So, let's say a cell can't make its DNA.

00:09:33.373 --> 00:09:37.076
Why does the cell stop

00:09:37.077 --> 00:09:40.046
with the chromosomes not separated?

00:09:40.047 --> 00:09:43.983
Why doesn't it just go ahead and try to separate the chromosomes,

00:09:43.984 --> 00:09:48.621
and get incomplete chromosomes and then die?

00:09:48.622 --> 00:09:50.189
Why does it stop?

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:51.357
How does it know?

00:09:51.358 --> 00:09:57.330
How does the mitotic spindle know that the DNA is incomplete?

00:09:57.331 --> 00:09:59.398
So, that was the question.

00:09:59.399 --> 00:10:04.270
And... and there's sort of two ways to think about this.

00:10:04.271 --> 00:10:10.510
One is that the DNA is part of the mitotic spindle,

00:10:10.511 --> 00:10:13.546
and if it's not complete then the spindle just can't function.

00:10:13.547 --> 00:10:15.781
So, that's sort of the substrate model.

00:10:15.782 --> 00:10:21.087
The other model is that there are signals in the cell.

00:10:21.088 --> 00:10:25.324
The cell has a communication system that tells it what's happening,

00:10:25.325 --> 00:10:27.026
and what hasn't happened.

00:10:27.027 --> 00:10:29.695
And that when the DNA isn't replicated,

00:10:29.696 --> 00:10:31.931
a signal is sent to the mitotic spindle

00:10:31.932 --> 00:10:34.367
that says wait, okay?

00:10:34.368 --> 00:10:36.769
So, that's the other model.

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:39.839
BIGGINS: So, which one did you discover was correct?

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.341
HARTWELL: So... yeah.

00:10:42.342 --> 00:10:45.912
So, that was a story

00:10:45.913 --> 00:10:50.683
that begins with a postdoctoral fellow named Ted Weinert.

00:10:50.684 --> 00:10:57.356
And Ted wanted to study regulation.

00:10:57.357 --> 00:11:04.897
And as we were discussing what regulation might mean,

00:11:04.898 --> 00:11:08.801
I had collected a bunch of radiation-sensitive mutants in the freezer.

00:11:08.802 --> 00:11:11.504
And so I got 'em out, and Ted looked at 'em.

00:11:11.505 --> 00:11:14.140
And it turned out that...

00:11:14.141 --> 00:11:17.109
I think it was just a few days...

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:21.447
that he discovered that some of the radiation-sensitive mutants,

00:11:21.448 --> 00:11:23.616
when you X-ray them,

00:11:23.617 --> 00:11:25.952
arrest cell cycle.

00:11:25.953 --> 00:11:29.255
So... and they just stay arrested,

00:11:29.256 --> 00:11:34.393
whereas the normal cell would arrest, repair, and then go on.

00:11:34.394 --> 00:11:38.331
So, these mutants were defective in the repair process.

00:11:38.332 --> 00:11:42.134
But there were other mutants that just didn't stop dividing.

00:11:42.135 --> 00:11:45.705
They just kept dividing and produced dead colonies.

00:11:45.706 --> 00:11:50.343
And so we realized that these were defective in the signaling.

00:11:50.344 --> 00:11:54.680
And that there was a signal that told the cell when there were breaks in DNA.

00:11:54.681 --> 00:11:56.482
BIGGINS: Right?

00:11:56.483 --> 00:11:58.184
And you defined this as a checkpoint? H

00:11:58.185 --> 00:11:59.118
ARTWELL: Yeah.

00:11:59.119 --> 00:12:01.187
BIGGINS: And do you want to say a little bit more about what you view

00:12:01.188 --> 00:12:03.656
the definition of a checkpoint as?

00:12:03.657 --> 00:12:09.695
HARTWELL: Well, we tried to define it relatively clearly,

00:12:09.696 --> 00:12:18.237
as... as something which is...

00:12:18.238 --> 00:12:23.776
is needed to arrest division when something goes wrong.

00:12:23.777 --> 00:12:26.345
BIGGINS: At the time, when you discovered this checkpoint,

00:12:26.346 --> 00:12:32.518
did you think that there might be other checkpoints for other cellular processes?

00:12:32.519 --> 00:12:35.755
HARTWELL: We certainly wondered that.

00:12:35.756 --> 00:12:42.562
And I think over the years,

00:12:42.563 --> 00:12:45.164
a lot more have been discovered.

00:12:45.165 --> 00:12:50.002
And so... you know,

00:12:50.003 --> 00:12:53.940
one of the, I think, really fundamental principles about biology,

00:12:53.941 --> 00:12:56.475
that fascinates me,

00:12:56.476 --> 00:13:00.179
is the tremendous accuracy of biological processes.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.249
So, yeast cells lose a chromosome

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.685
about once in 100,000 divisions.

00:13:05.686 --> 00:13:11.224
That's remarkably precise and reproducible,

00:13:11.225 --> 00:13:14.427
especially when you watch mitosis

00:13:14.428 --> 00:13:16.596
and see the chromosomes jiggling around and everything.

00:13:16.597 --> 00:13:20.099
You wonder how they keep track of them.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:24.504
And I suspect that's true for all kinds of cellular processes,

00:13:24.505 --> 00:13:28.174
that normally we can't measure the accuracy

00:13:28.175 --> 00:13:30.176
because it is so accurate.

00:13:30.177 --> 00:13:37.316
And... and so the extent to which evolution

00:13:37.317 --> 00:13:41.921
has driven the accuracy of biological processes

00:13:41.922 --> 00:13:43.689
is really enormous.

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.058
And it... I think it must mean that,

00:13:46.059 --> 00:13:49.962
for most things that go on in biology,

00:13:49.963 --> 00:13:53.566
there's the basic machinery,

00:13:53.567 --> 00:13:57.803
there are things which repair the basic machinery when it gets in trouble,

00:13:57.804 --> 00:14:01.507
and there are things that coordinate that repair

00:14:01.508 --> 00:14:03.776
with everything else that's going on.

00:14:03.777 --> 00:14:08.181
And we probably only know the tip of the iceberg yet

00:14:08.182 --> 00:14:10.650
in terms of cell biology.

00:14:10.651 --> 00:14:15.755
BIGGINS: cdc28 is the most famous, conserved

00:14:15.756 --> 00:14:17.924
cell cycle molecule, probably,

00:14:17.925 --> 00:14:21.460
since that was in large part...

00:14:21.461 --> 00:14:27.300
winning the Nobel Prize with Tim Hunt and Paul Nurse's contributions.

00:14:27.301 --> 00:14:30.369
What did you think... before we knew what that...

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:33.472
what that protein did, did you have a feeling,

00:14:33.473 --> 00:14:37.443
what you thought that molecule would end up being?

00:14:37.444 --> 00:14:41.147
HARTWELL: No, I really didn't think in terms of biochemistry.

00:14:41.148 --> 00:14:43.649
I wasn't interested in biochemistry.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:45.952
It's funny.

00:14:45.953 --> 00:14:49.455
But I was just much more interested

00:14:49.456 --> 00:14:55.061
in the order and and regulation of events in the cell cycle.

00:14:55.062 --> 00:15:03.769
But it's really interesting that before that story broke,

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:11.077
Fischer and Krebs, who had gotten the Nobel Prize for protein phosphorylation,

00:15:11.078 --> 00:15:14.614
came to me and said... and we had a meeting,

00:15:14.615 --> 00:15:17.183
and they wanted to know whether I thought kinases

00:15:17.184 --> 00:15:20.019
could play any role in the cell cycle.

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:21.821
And I said, well, you know,

00:15:21.822 --> 00:15:23.823
I just don't know, you know?

00:15:23.824 --> 00:15:27.226
But it was interesting, because that was actually the right guess.

00:15:27.227 --> 00:15:30.062
And cdc28 turned out to be a kinase,

00:15:30.063 --> 00:15:34.834
and their suspicions were correct.

00:15:34.835 --> 00:15:38.538
BIGGINS: We now know that many of the CDC genes are conserved?

00:15:38.539 --> 00:15:42.475
Was it a surprise to you how many were conserved,

00:15:42.476 --> 00:15:44.844
now that we know what they all are?

00:15:44.845 --> 00:15:47.213
Were more conserved than you thought? Less?

00:15:47.214 --> 00:15:51.817
HARTWELL: I think, in general,

00:15:51.818 --> 00:15:55.488
biology has surprised us all in the degree of conservation

00:15:55.489 --> 00:15:58.624
at the molecular level.

00:15:58.625 --> 00:16:03.596
And we're even seeing...

00:16:03.597 --> 00:16:04.463
so, the... so, the answer is yes, for me.

00:16:04.464 --> 00:16:06.232
It surprised me.

00:16:06.233 --> 00:16:09.202
I was just hoping that cell division in yeast

00:16:09.203 --> 00:16:12.672
would be enough like mammalian cells to be a useful model.

00:16:12.673 --> 00:16:17.210
And only after genes were cloned and sequenced

00:16:17.211 --> 00:16:20.079
and various things

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:23.049
did we find out how conserved they are.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:28.654
I think it's something like 50% of yeast genes

00:16:28.655 --> 00:16:33.226
can be complemented by human proteins.

00:16:33.227 --> 00:16:36.062
BIGGINS: I think it's amazing what you are able to learn

00:16:36.063 --> 00:16:39.899
given how little you could really measure.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:43.302
Now we have so many tools to

00:16:43.303 --> 00:16:46.038
know what's happening to different structures in the cell.

00:16:46.039 --> 00:16:48.374
And you were really limited to just looking at budding

00:16:48.375 --> 00:16:52.144
and the nuclear position.

00:16:52.145 --> 00:16:55.681
I'm curious if there was, at the time,

00:16:55.682 --> 00:16:58.451
one extra thing you could have seen

00:16:58.452 --> 00:17:00.453
or one tool you could have had,

00:17:00.454 --> 00:17:02.054
what would you have chosen?

00:17:02.055 --> 00:17:04.457
What would have given you the most information

00:17:04.458 --> 00:17:06.125
that you couldn't get at that time?

00:17:06.126 --> 00:17:08.094
HARTWELL: Well, we actually had that experience,

00:17:08.095 --> 00:17:13.165
in that when we first learned to do photomicroscopy,

00:17:13.166 --> 00:17:15.401
and could look at the morphology of the cells

00:17:15.402 --> 00:17:19.105
and pick out those that looked like they might be cell cycle mutants,

00:17:19.106 --> 00:17:22.909
we didn't know what was going on inside the cell.

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:25.878
So, we had to be able to see the nucleus.

00:17:25.879 --> 00:17:28.014
And at that time, that was very difficult,

00:17:28.015 --> 00:17:30.716
to stain the cells to see the nucleus.

00:17:30.717 --> 00:17:33.252
And there was only one guy who could really do it.

00:17:33.253 --> 00:17:34.820
His name was Robineaux.

00:17:34.821 --> 00:17:39.225
And it turned out there was a... some international meeting,

00:17:39.226 --> 00:17:41.294
and he was coming to Seattle.

00:17:41.295 --> 00:17:44.864
And I got in touch with him and asked him if he'd come to the lab a day ahead

00:17:44.865 --> 00:17:47.733
and show us how to see nuclei and stain them.

00:17:47.734 --> 00:17:49.468
And he did.

00:17:49.469 --> 00:17:52.038
And so, then we were able to verify

00:17:52.039 --> 00:17:53.840
that the mutants that looked like cell division mutants

00:17:53.841 --> 00:18:00.012
were actually defective in nuclear division as well.

00:18:00.013 --> 00:18:03.382
BIGGINS: What was so hard about getting the nuclear staining to work?

00:18:03.383 --> 00:18:07.954
HARTWELL: It was just tricky

00:18:07.955 --> 00:18:13.092
because the amount of DNA is so small in the yeast.

00:18:13.093 --> 00:18:16.329
And you're trying to stain the DNA.

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:20.266
And there's 100 times as much RNA as there is DNA.

00:18:20.267 --> 00:18:26.072
And so it's hard to get the staining conditions just right

00:18:26.073 --> 00:18:28.140
so you can see that DNA.

00:18:28.141 --> 00:18:30.543
BIGGINS: So, if there could have been one more structure you could see,

00:18:30.544 --> 00:18:32.778
what would you have chosen at the time?

00:18:32.779 --> 00:18:34.714
HARTWELL: Oh, well that's an interesting story too,

00:18:34.715 --> 00:18:37.316
now that you bring it up.

00:18:37.317 --> 00:18:41.821
My colleague, Breck Byers,

00:18:41.822 --> 00:18:45.558
was in the genetics department.

00:18:45.559 --> 00:18:47.360
And I went to him at one time

00:18:47.361 --> 00:18:49.428
and said, well, we've got all these mutants.

00:18:49.429 --> 00:18:53.966
We should, you know, look at their detailed three-dimensional structure.

00:18:53.967 --> 00:18:56.869
Do thin sections and reconstruct the cell and everything.

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:58.704
And he looked at me, and he said,

00:18:58.705 --> 00:19:00.306
there's only one thing of interest.

00:19:00.307 --> 00:19:02.975
And that's the spindle.

00:19:02.976 --> 00:19:05.144
And I said, okay.

00:19:05.145 --> 00:19:08.214
So, he started looking at the spindle on electron microscopy,

00:19:08.215 --> 00:19:12.051
and found that some mutants were defective in making the pole duplicate,

00:19:12.052 --> 00:19:14.921
and some others were defective in forming the spindle.

00:19:14.922 --> 00:19:18.057
BIGGINS: You mentioned earlier, and in your Nobel lecture as well,

00:19:18.058 --> 00:19:19.959
that when you started your lab

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.028
you didn't really have a plan of what you were gonna work on.

00:19:22.029 --> 00:19:24.564
And this is so different than how things work today,

00:19:24.565 --> 00:19:27.533
where people have to have

00:19:27.534 --> 00:19:31.504
brilliant, well-developed research proposals to get a job,

00:19:31.505 --> 00:19:34.307
and they know exactly what they're gonna work on.

00:19:34.308 --> 00:19:36.609
And I'm just curious if you think this change

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:39.111
actually hinders creativity,

00:19:39.112 --> 00:19:42.281
or if this is a good change.

00:19:42.282 --> 00:19:45.384
HARTWELL: I think the present system does hinder creativity.

00:19:45.385 --> 00:19:54.760
I think that opening up new fields

00:19:54.761 --> 00:20:01.000
requires a long incubation period.

00:20:01.001 --> 00:20:03.102
I just recently experienced it myself,

00:20:03.103 --> 00:20:09.242
because the last decade I've been interested in education.

00:20:09.243 --> 00:20:12.144
And it's taken me ten years

00:20:12.145 --> 00:20:15.014
to sort of figure out what I wanna do there.

00:20:15.015 --> 00:20:17.984
And the current system

00:20:17.985 --> 00:20:21.387
just doesn't allow students

00:20:21.388 --> 00:20:24.023
to go beyond their immediate experience.

00:20:24.024 --> 00:20:27.026
So, you can keep extending

00:20:27.027 --> 00:20:29.595
what's already going on and what's interesting and productive,

00:20:29.596 --> 00:20:32.431
but to really start something new

00:20:32.432 --> 00:20:36.369
is very hard to do in the current environment.

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:38.871
Established labs can do it

00:20:38.872 --> 00:20:42.074
if they have a little bit of leeway to try something new

00:20:42.075 --> 00:20:43.609
for a year or something.

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:46.946
But... that and the whole system of,

00:20:46.947 --> 00:20:52.852
you know, counting publications, and impact factors, and grants,

00:20:52.853 --> 00:20:54.320
and all the things.

00:20:54.321 --> 00:20:56.389
I think the best system for sponsoring science

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:59.892
was the old system at the MRC, in England,

00:20:59.893 --> 00:21:01.360
which doesn't exist anymore.

00:21:01.361 --> 00:21:03.963
The MRC exists but the system doesn't exist.

00:21:03.964 --> 00:21:06.999
They've adopted the American system unfortunately.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:10.636
But their scientists used to be given

00:21:10.637 --> 00:21:14.240
a budget, yearly,

00:21:14.241 --> 00:21:16.075
that would support themselves and a technician

00:21:16.076 --> 00:21:18.444
and maybe a graduate student or two.

00:21:18.445 --> 00:21:20.780
And that was guaranteed,

00:21:20.781 --> 00:21:24.917
regardless of whether they produced anything or not.

00:21:24.918 --> 00:21:28.054
And then, they could give grants on top of that.

00:21:28.055 --> 00:21:31.524
But I think that basic level of stability

00:21:31.525 --> 00:21:34.026
allowed some people to do very creative things

00:21:34.027 --> 00:21:53.247
and take five years to do it before they published.

